 **Pitfalls:**
 - **Light Direction Assumption:** The light direction computation assumes the object is at the origin. If the object is offset, the lighting will be incorrect [S1, unofficial].
-- **Precision:** Ensure tensor device placement matches the model's device to avoid runtime errors [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the neural network forward pass. For $N$ rays and model complexity $M$, time is $O(N \cdot M)$.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot 3)$ for storing ray origins and directions, plus $O(N \cdot 3)$ for the output tensor [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** End-to-end rendering pipeline; supports arbitrary neural implicit representations [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Dependent on the efficiency of the underlying neural model; lighting calculations are simplified and assume origin-centered scenes [S1, unofficial].
+The sources do not specify time or memory complexity, measured performance, or scaling [S1, unofficial]. Strengths include end-to-end rendering of implicit features [S1, unofficial]. Limitations include simplified lighting and origin-centered assumptions [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This pipeline is part of the Neural Surfaces project, which uses neural networks to render implicit surfaces [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the [Grid Ray Sampler](3930-grid-ray-sampler.md) for ray generation.
